INT. RARE BOOKS READING ROOM - NIGHT

Oak tables. Green-shaded lamps. Fire doors sealed against the cold. Behind a glass wall, shelves of locked cases rise into shadow.

CATALINA ROJAS, 40s, head librarian, sits at the desk with a cotton glove on each hand. She is turning the pages of a ledger and does not look up. She is calm, orderly, someone whose life has been alphabetical.

Across the room, VIKRAM SHARMA, 30s, a visiting scholar in a corduroy jacket, hunches over a folio with a magnifying glass. A laptop sits open beside him. He has been reading the same page for an hour.

By the door, ELLSWORTH DUVAL, 50s, security, in a navy blazer, leans on the wall and checks his phone. He yawns.

She glances at the clock. Closing in ten minutes. She stands, collects a tray, and walks the aisle to Vikram's table. He covers the folio with his arm before she reaches it.

She stops. She smiles. He smiles back, too quickly.

CATALINA
We close at ten, Dr. Sharma.

VIKRAM
Of course. Just finishing.

CATALINA
Is it a good page?

VIKRAM
It's a page.

She sets the tray down beside him. She does not take the folio. Instead she turns to Ellsworth and nods, a small, precise tilt of the head. He puts away his phone and straightens.

Vikram sees it. His hand moves under the table.

CATALINA (CONT'D)
That's a first edition. Seventeen-twelve. We've had it since the war.

VIKRAM
It's magnificent.

CATALINA
Plate nine is missing.

Vikram doesn't answer. He lifts his arm. The folio opens to a clean stub where a plate has been razored out.

VIKRAM
It was missing when I got it.

CATALINA
It was in the case at four.

Ellsworth crosses the floor in six long steps. Vikram stands. His chair crashes backward. His hand comes out from under the table with a short folding knife. Ellsworth halts.

Nobody breathes. Catalina does not move. The lamp hums.

She speaks gently, as if to a child.

CATALINA (CONT'D)
Put it on the table, Dr. Sharma.

For a long beat he stares at her. Then his arm drops. The knife clatters onto the oak. Ellsworth takes his wrist and turns him. Vikram does not resist.

Catalina takes the folio. She smooths the stub with a gloved fingertip.

CATALINA (CONT'D)
Where is it?

VIKRAM
(hoarse)
In my laptop sleeve.

She nods. Ellsworth fishes the plate out and hands it to her. She lays it flat between two sheets of tissue.

The tension drains from the room. Ellsworth sighs. Vikram slumps. Catalina closes the folio and turns off the lamp.
